## C6 submission: Split-flag bypass of destructive command detector

Claude Code's dangerous-rm detector matches `rm -rf` as a combined string. Splitting flags passes ungated:

- `rm -r -f /target` → NOT BLOCKED (identical effect)
- `rm -f -r /target` → NOT BLOCKED
- `rm -fr /target` → NOT BLOCKED
- `rm --recursive --force /target` → NOT BLOCKED
- `rm -r --force /target` → NOT BLOCKED
- `sh -c "rm -r -f /target"` → NOT BLOCKED

**Why:** The detector uses `cmdString.includes('rm -rf')` — a substring check. GNU rm treats `-r -f`, `-fr`, `-rf`, and `--recursive --force` as identical.

**Fix — PreToolUse hook that tokenizes flags:**

```javascript
// Hook: check-destructive-rm.js
const RECURSIVE = new Set(['-r', '-R', '--recursive']);
const FORCE = new Set(['-f', '--force']);

function isDangerousRm(args) {
  const rmIdx = args.findIndex(a => a === 'rm');
  if (rmIdx === -1) return false;
  const flags = [];
  for (let i = rmIdx + 1; i < args.length; i++) {
    const a = args[i];
    if (a === '--') break;
    if (a.startsWith('--')) flags.push(a);
    else for (let j = 1; j < a.length; j++) flags.push('-' + a[j]);
  }
  const hasR = flags.some(f => RECURSIVE.has(f));
  const hasF = flags.some(f => FORCE.has(f));
  return hasR && hasF;
}
```

**15/15 tests passing:**
All split-flag variants → DENIED
Single -f, single -r → ALLOWED
sh -c wrapping → DENIED
No-target edge case → ALLOWED

**Residual:** Shell variable expansion ($FORCE, $(echo -rf)) not resolved at string level.
